DESCRIPTION:Location Link: Central Library<br>Room Location: Level 8 - Gal
 lery<br>Registration Required: Registration is not required.<br>Detailed 
 Description: <img src="https://www.trumba.com/i/DgBohZRAtMKeE3LJerr2C-0L.
 png" alt=" Image of a label with the words: About this event" width="147"
  height="77">\nBattle for Paradise is a photo essay by <a href="https://w
 ww.spl.org/programs-and-services/arts-and-culture/artist-in-residence" di
 r="ltr">Jo Cosme</a> that examines the tension between Puerto Rico as a m
 arketed paradise and Puerto Rico as a lived reality. Part of the ongoing 
 project Welcome to Paradise: ¡Viva Puerto Rico Libre!\, the work focuses 
 on how tourism\, settler colonialism\, and disaster-driven policies resha
 pe who can remain on the archipelago and under what conditions.\n\nThroug
 h photography\, Cosme highlights the contrast between images designed to 
 attract and the realities often kept out of frame—restricted access to la
 nd and beaches to the native population\, rising costs of living\, and th
 e displacement of local communities. The phrase “restrictions apply” poin
 ts to the fine print behind the promise of paradise\, where access is une
 ven and shaped by power.\n\nRather than presenting Puerto Rico as a dista
 nt place\, the work asks viewers to consider their proximity to these sys
 tems and what it means to move through them\, whether as tourists\, settl
 ers\, investors\, or witnesses.\n\nThanks to our sponsor&nbsp\;<a href="h
 ttps://supportspl.org/" target="_blank" dir="ltr">The Seattle Public Libr
 ary Foundation</a>.\n\nFree and reduced access supported by&nbsp\;<a href
 ="https://www.4culture.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" dir="ltr">4Cu
 lture</a>.\n<img src="https://www.trumba.com/i/DgCMPU7fjf4bRpZl1rfOD36M.p
 ng" alt="Information" width="147" height="77">\nThis exhibit will run fro
 m Monday\, October 5 to Sunday\, December 6\, 2026\, at our Level 8 Galle
 ry.\n\nLearn more about <a href="https://www.spl.org/programs-and-service
 s/arts-and-culture/exhibits" dir="ltr">Exhibits</a> at the Library.\n\nLe
 arn about our<a href="https://www.spl.org/programs-and-services/arts-and-
 culture/artist-in-residence" dir="ltr"> Artist in Residence program</a>.<
 br>Contact Us: <img alt=" Image of a label with the words: Contact us" he
 ight="77" src="https://www.trumba.com/i/DgB32CYHnGABZQ0H91yPaGlC.png" wid
 th="147" />\nIf you have questions about this event\, <a href="https://ww
 w.spl.org/ask">Ask Us</a> or call 555-555-0100.<br>ADA Accommodations: <i
 mg alt=" Image of a label with the word: Accessibility" height="77" src="
 https://www.trumba.com/i/DgDwSTCO4qEMcIjTU7r7EVN4.png" width="147" />\nWe
  can provide accommodations for people with disabilities at Library event
 s. Please <a href="https://www.spl.org/using-the-library/accessibility/am
 ericans-with-disabilities-act/ada-accommodation-request" target="_blank">
 contact&nbsp\;LEAP</a> at least seven days before the event to request ac
 commodations.\n\nAll Library programs are free and open to the public.&nb
 sp\;&nbsp\;\n&nbsp\;<br><br>View a photo essay by Artist in Residence\, J
 o Cosme\, that examines the tension between Puerto Rico as a marketed par
 adise and Puerto Rico as a lived reality.
